COMPLETE FIX (diagnosed + mechanism-verified; NOT landed — it's core parse/fmt, inside the ps3t AST-rewrite zone, so handing off). Two parts:

PART 1 — fmt/mod.av:47 (the EnumCtor arm drops the type qualifier):
  BEFORE: .EnumCtor(tn, variant, args) -> ".${variant}(${fmt_expr_ids_comma(store, args)})"
  AFTER:  .EnumCtor(tn, variant, args) -> "${tn}.${variant}(${fmt_expr_ids_comma(store, args)})"
  (tn is "" for a genuinely subjectless .Variant, so byte-identical there; only ADDS the qualifier where dropped. Matches render_expr, which already qualifies.)
  VERIFIED: after this, the regenerated std-cli unit.meta.bin serializes the @comptime bodies with qualified ctors, e.g. "@std::avrac::core::Expr.StructLit(...)" and "@std::avrac::core::Stmt.Let(...)" — where before it was ".StructLit(...)" / ".Let(...)".

PART 2 — parse/mod.av (~line 1491): fmt part alone still F4006's, because the enum-ctor gate only fires for a BARE PascalCase Ident. A fully-qualified type falls through to Call(FieldAccess(QualifiedIdent, variant), args), which eval mishandles (the FieldAccess->type_marker path builds a ZERO-arg enum, ignoring the real args) -> macro returns null. Add a QualifiedIdent arm to the `type_name` match:
    .QualifiedIdent(path) -> {
        if p_starts_with_upper(p_last_path_segment(path)) { path } else { "" }
    }
  plus a helper (p_starts_with_upper checks the FIRST char, which is "@" for a qualified path, so check the LAST ::-segment):
    fn p_last_path_segment(path: string) -> string {
        mut last = 0
        mut i = 0
        while i + 1 < path.length {
            if path.substring(i, i + 2) == "::" { last = i + 2 }
            i = i + 1
        }
        path.substring(last, path.length)
    }

NOT run end-to-end (the rebuild-with-both-parts was stopped). Part 1 is confirmed; Part 2 is the confirmed logical completion (the parser gap is read-verified at 1491). No eval change needed: eval already handles EnumCtor with a qualified type_name (source mode does exactly this post-rewrite_uses). Both edits are core parse/fmt — ps3t's active AST-rewrite zone — so land them inside that rewrite rather than as a vez6 consumer patch. Unblocks vez6.12 (the flip compiles via the metadata fast-path).

design:
REPRO (minimal): a fixture with a match-arm-position splice via `~arms`:
  @comptime fn make_arms() -> List<MatchArm> { [quote arm {1->100}, quote arm {2->200}] }
  @comptime fn build_main() -> List<SStmt> { let arms=make_arms(); quote stmts { let v=2; let r=match v { ~arms _ -> 0 }; println(string(r)) } }
  @expand(build_main) fn placeholder() {}
Run through any parse_or_fail path WITH ingest enabled (i.e. the candidate arena-canonical change that calls ingest_program in parse_or_fail unconditionally). Crashes:
  error: non-exhaustive match in Expr__all_children_spanned — unmatched tag <HEAP PTR> (looks like a heap ptr; bytes don't match List<T>)

NARROWING: simple `quote stmts { ... }` and `quote stmts { ... match v { 2->200 _->0 } ... }` (no arm-splice) do NOT crash. ONLY the `~arms` match-arm-position splice triggers it. Existing test: features/quote_expr/tests/quote_splice_arm_list_test.av (passes today only because ingest is gated OFF by default).

SENTINEL: parse_match_arm_list emits MatchArm { pattern: TypePattern("__quote_splice_arms__", ""), body: Splice(target) } (features/quote_expr/lower.av:622-623). MatchArm is struct-embedded (classified Scalar -> NOT walked by all_children_spanned per the v8op boundary), so how the forward walk reaches/mis-reads it is the open question.

SIGNATURE: 'unmatched tag = heap ptr' is the documented memory-corruption pattern (CLAUDE.md RC_MEMORY_RUNBOOK), NOT a clean missing-variant. Per the debugging protocol: LLDB first (do NOT guess). Candidate angles: (a) is the Splice/quote body mis-ingested producing a wrong-shape Expr? (b) AVRA_VERIFY_RC=1 / AVRA_REDZONES=1; (c) seed-diff Expr__all_children_spanned.

WHY IT MATTERS: blocks y5um Phase 1 (make the arena canonical on the default parse_or_fail path). The candidate change was diff-test BYTE-IDENTICAL on the selfhost+corpus (which don't use ~arms) but crashed 1 spec (quote_splice_arm_list). Fix ingest/all_children_spanned robustness to ALL parser outputs, then ungate.

description:
Deep-probe finding (63wu arc continuation). check_method_call's user-defined-method branch (typeck/mod.av, the 'if r.found' block) returns the substituted ret type but NEVER validates the call args against the method's declared param types (r.params, self at [0], user args align with params[1..]). So BOTH a generic-arg layout conflict and a bare scalar mismatch slip through as wild stores:
  type Box<T> = { val: T }; type Holder = { x: int }
  impl Holder { fn take(self, b: Box<string>) -> int { self.x } }
  Holder{x:1}.take(Box{val:9})   // Box<int> into Box<string> param — compiles clean, SHOULD be F1000
  impl Holder { fn take(self, s: string) -> int {...} }; h.take(9)  // int into string param — compiles clean, SHOULD be F1000
Free functions ARE checked (tc_check_newtype_args, lines ~2281-2297, runs generic_args_layout_conflict + int_into_pointer_slot per arg) — methods just bypass that path. Fix: in the r.found branch, loop user args against params[1..] reusing the SAME two predicates (settled/per-pair guard makes it safe for still-generic method type params). Generic methods (Holder<T>.add(x:T)) are the same fix (W5). High value — method calls are ubiquitous.

Type-param names are resolved against the global type registry instead of being local bindings that shadow type names. Two symptoms of the same root cause:

1. A generic fn whose type-param shares a name with a TYPE in scope fails to bind:
     export fn map_node<W, T>(x: W, f: fn(T) -> T) -> W { x with { node: f(x.node) } }
     // user enum literally named T:
     @expand(derive_walker) export enum T { Leaf(v:int), One(child: SN) }   // SN = { node: T, span: int }
     // derive_walker emits map_node::<SN, T>(child, f) -> F1002 'cannot infer type parameter T in map_node'
   The explicit type-arg 'T' (the enum) clashes with map_node's own param 'T'.

2. Renaming the param to a name that IS a (real or phantom) type leaks the UNBOUND param as that type:
     map_node<W, Node>  ->  unwrap_sstmt_list F1000 'returns List<Stmt> but body produces List<@std::avrac::core::Node>'
   (No type 'Node' exists — the unbound type-param was printed/resolved as a qualified type name.)

So NO param name is safe; renaming is not a fix. The fix is in typeck: a fn's type-params must be a local scope that shadows type names, and explicit type-args must resolve in the CALLER's scope.

Affects the whole wrapper-helper family (unwrap_node_list / map_node_list — merged via #10 — and map_node, #12) and, by extension, any generic call whose type-arg/param names collide with a type. The bootstrap compiler is unaffected (its walked enums are Stmt/Expr/ValueType/Pattern, none named T), so it's an adversarial USER edge; selfhost stays green. Surfaced during PR #12's aggressive review. NOT introduced by #12 — map_node<W,T> is consistent with the already-merged map_node_list<W,T>.

Every error type / struct currently writes its own `.message()` / `.to_string()` method and callers spell it out at every interpolation:

    println("error: ${err.message()}")
    println("user: ${u.to_string()}")

If `${expr}` in a string literal desugared to `${expr.display()}` (calling a stdlib `Display` trait), every type becomes interpolatable:

    println("error: ${err}")        // calls IsolatedError.display()
    println("user: ${u}")           // calls User.display()
    println("count: ${42}")         // primitives have impl Display
    println("items: ${items}")      // List<T: Display> has blanket impl

## Surface

    trait Display { fn display(self) -> string }

    // Primitive impls in core/builtin
    impl Display for int { fn display(self) -> string { int_to_string(self) } }
    impl Display for string { fn display(self) -> string { self } }
    impl Display for bool { fn display(self) -> string { if self { "true" } else { "false" } } }
    impl Display for float { fn display(self) -> string { float_to_string(self) } }

    // User types opt in:
    impl Display for IsolatedError {
        fn display(self) -> string {
            match self { .Crashed -> "child crashed", ... }
        }
    }

## Implementation

1. Add `Display` trait to `@std/core` (or wherever Result lives).
2. Define `impl Display for int / string / bool / float` in core.
3. Parser: `${expr}` in InterpString stays parsed as before.
4. Codegen: emit a `Display.display(expr)` trait dispatch instead of relying on string coercion. Today's string-coercion path stays as fallback for backwards compat.
5. Diagnostic: if T doesn't impl Display, error at the `${value}` site with a helpful "add `impl Display for T`" message.

## Acceptance

- `println("err: ${IsolatedError.Crashed}")` works.
- `println("user: ${User { id: 1, name: \"alice\" }}")` works when `impl Display for User` exists.
- Without impl: F1xxx error at the interpolation site, not a silent fallthrough.
- Every existing `${expr}` site keeps working (primitives have impls).
- Drop `.message()` from IsolatedError / MarshalError / etc. — replace with `impl Display`.

## Drives

The cheapest big win across the codebase. Every error / struct / list / map drops its bespoke to_string method. Mirrors Rust `std::fmt::Display`, Swift `CustomStringConvertible`, Go `Stringer`, Haskell `Show`.

When a @comptime fn body contains a `match` statement on its arg, eval fails with: 'eval error: match statements are not supported by the tree-walk evaluator'.

Repro:
```
@comptime
fn expand_command(s: Stmt) -> Stmt {
    match s {
        .ComponentBlock(_, instance, _, _) -> {
            return Stmt.TypeDecl("Command_" + instance, ..., ...)
        }
        _ -> return s
    }
}
```

This blocks the canonical vez6.8 macro pattern: `@expand` macros receive the wrapped Stmt and need to pattern-match to extract the instance name + body. Without match support, every macro is forced into ugly if-let-style chains or is_X(s) helper boolean checks — assuming those are supported either.

Surfaced while testing vez6.8 end-to-end per Stage 3 of GETTING_TO_CLI_V2.md.

The eval module (features/eval/mod.av or similar) handles tree-walk evaluation of @comptime bodies. It needs a Match arm in eval_stmt (or eval_expr for match-as-expression) that:
1. Evaluates the subject
2. Walks each arm, attempting to bind the pattern
3. On first matching arm, evaluates the body in extended bindings
4. On no match (and no wildcard), errors

Pattern types needed initially:
- .EnumCtor(variant_name, fields) destructuring (the most common case)
- _ wildcard
- Identifier binding (for match arms that just bind the whole subject)

Effort: 1-2 days. eval already handles most expressions; match is structurally similar — pattern compile + branch dispatch over the runtime Value. The Value type already has enum_type/enum_variant/enum_payload, so destructuring is a tag check + payload index walk.

Rewrite the bootstrap compiler's CLI (currently hardcoded argument parsing in src/main.fg) to use the new @std/cli library from ticket dz8e.

Current CLI in main.fg is ~100 lines of manual string comparison:
  if arg == "compile" { ... }
  if arg == "--coverage" { ... }
  etc.

Replace with clean @std/cli usage:
  cli("forge") {
    command("compile") { arg("file") ... }
    command("run") { arg("file") ... }
    command("check") { arg("file") ... }
    command("test") { ... }
    ...
  }

This ticket depends on dz8e (std-cli port) being complete.

The end goal is a clean, well-structured CLI that serves as a real-world dogfood test of the @std/cli library.

IMPORTANT: Do NOT work around any language limitations. If a feature is missing, pause and implement it first. No hacks, no workarounds.

Repro:
  type Box = { val: int }
  fn main() { let o: Box? = .Some(Box { val: 9 }) }
typeck accepts it (the .Optional present-ctor path), but codegen ICEs with F9999: 'codegen error: no enum has variant Some'. The subjectless .Some ctor is not resolved to the Optional enum's Some variant when the optional inner is a user type (Box). Pre-existing and orthogonal to typeck — discovered while testing 98z0. The enwi typeck tests deliberately drive .Some cases via 'bs2 check' (not compile) to avoid this codegen gap; a positive control in ctor_scalar_layout_conflict_test.av hit it via compile and was removed (the legit .Some typeck path stays covered by enwi). Fix belongs in codegen's subjectless-ctor resolution: resolve a bare .Some/.None against the expected Optional type. Related: enwi (typeck inference for the same form).

Deep-probe finding (63wu arc). A BARE (non-leading-dot) Result/Option constructor call reaches codegen and ICEs with F9999 "internal compiler error during codegen: codegen error: undefined function `Ok`" (also Err, Some).

Repro:
  type Box<T> = { val: T }
  fn f() -> Result<Box<string>, string> { return Ok(Box { val: 9 }) }   // F9999
  fn main() { let r = f() }
  // and: fn main() { let o: Box<string>? = Some(Box { val: 9 }) }       // F9999

Contrast: a TOTALLY undefined function (totally_undefined_fn(9)) correctly gives a clean F3000 at resolution. So the general undefined-call path is fine; only the special names Ok/Err/Some leak to codegen. Root cause: these names are half-recognized via is_result_enum_name / variant_find(variants, "Ok") in typeck, so resolution does not reject them, but codegen has no emit path for a bare Ok(...) (the only valid form is the leading-dot enum ctor .Ok(...), of which there are 476 uses in source).

ICE-policy violation (CLAUDE.md: F9999 must be unreachable from user input). Two acceptable fixes: (a) desugar bare Ok/Err/Some to the leading-dot enum ctor (.Ok/.Err/.Some) when the Result/Option enum is in scope, OR (b) reject them at resolution with a clean F3000 + help text suggesting .Ok. This is a "make codegen total / typeck gates codegen" instance: codegen's "undefined function" branch should be unreachable, not an ICE.

Deep-probe finding (63wu arc). A closure stored in a local var has its RESULT type checked (let f = (b: string) -> b; let x: int = f("a") IS F1000), but its ARGUMENTS are not checked against the closure's parameter types.

Repro (both compile clean, SHOULD be F1000):
  type Box<T> = { val: T }
  fn main() {
    let f = (b: Box<string>) -> b
    let _ = f(Box { val: 9 })        // Box<int> into the closure's Box<string> param
  }
  // and the scalar variant:
  fn main() { let g = (s: string) -> s; let _ = g(9) }   // int into string param

The .Call path checks args against REGISTERED free-fn params (tc_check_newtype_args) but not against a local closure var's FnTyped/Closure param types. Fix: when the callee resolves to a local binding of FnTyped(params, ret), run the same generic_args_layout_conflict + int_into_pointer_slot per arg. Depends on the closure binding carrying its param types (FnTyped); verify the binding's ValueType and thread params if currently elided to Fn(ret).

Related: W7 (a fn/closure that RETURNS a closure whose body conflicts with the declared closure return type is also unchecked). Both are closure-arg/closure-body checking gaps. A centralized assignability gate (nlzq) + full bidirectional threading would cover these by construction.

Deep-probe finding (63wu arc). generic_args_layout_conflict (typeck/mod.av) has no .FnTyped branch, so passing a function value whose signature is layout-incompatible with the expected fn-typed parameter is undetected.

Repro (compiles clean, SHOULD be F1000):
  type Box<T> = { val: T }
  fn apply(f: fn(Box<string>) -> Box<string>, x: Box<string>) -> Box<string> { f(x) }
  fn g(b: Box<int>) -> Box<int> { b }
  fn main() { let _ = apply(g, Box { val: "ok" }) }

Why it is a real wild store: apply() calls f(some Box<string>), but g reads its arg's .val field as an int when it is actually a string pointer -> derefs a string-ptr as int.

Fix: add an .FnTyped(pa, ra) vs .FnTyped(pb, rb) branch to generic_args_layout_conflict that recurses pairwise over the param list (reuse type_list_layout_conflict) and over the return type. Once the predicate handles FnTyped, the existing free-fn arg check (tc_check_newtype_args) catches arg-position cases, and let/return/field positions get it for free through the shared predicate.

NOTE: this is one of several positions a single centralized assignability gate (nlzq) would cover by construction.

Deep-probe finding (63wu arc). generic_args_layout_conflict has no .FnTyped branch, so passing a function value whose signature is layout-incompatible with the expected fn-typed parameter is undetected:
  type Box<T> = { val: T }
  fn apply(f: fn(Box<string>) -> Box<string>, x: Box<string>) -> Box<string> { f(x) }
  fn g(b: Box<int>) -> Box<int> { b }
  apply(g, Box{val:"ok"})   // fn(Box<int>) into fn(Box<string>) slot — compiles clean, SHOULD be F1000
This is a real layout hole: apply() calls f(some_Box_string) but g reads its arg's .val as an int → derefs a string-ptr as int. Fix: add an .FnTyped(pa,ra) vs .FnTyped(pb,rb) branch to generic_args_layout_conflict recursing pairwise over params (type_list_layout_conflict) + ret. The existing free-fn arg check (tc_check_newtype_args) then catches arg-position cases; let/return/field positions get it for free via the shared predicate.

A type alias used as a type-pattern is not resolved to its target, so the bound var keeps the alias type and member access ICEs:

    type MyStr = string
    fn f(v: MyInt | MyStr) -> int { match v { MyInt(n) -> n, MyStr(s) -> s.length } }
    // F9999 codegen: field access on non-struct value (ty=MyStr, field=length)

rewrite_pattern resolves TypePattern names via resolve_type_in_ctx, but that doesn't resolve aliases (MyStr -> string). Pre-existing; surfaced while preparing PR #10. Related: generic type-alias 'type Box<T> = T' also mishandled (F1000 body produces T vs returns Box<T>).

A generic fn whose return type buries the type-param inside a Tuple or Union ICEs at codegen:

    fn pair<T>(x: T) -> (T, int) { (x, 0) }
    fn main() { let p = pair("hi"); println(p.0) }
    // F9999: codegen error: undefined variable `T` in function `__bs_top_level`

The return type's T is not fully substituted before codegen, so T leaks in as a 'variable'. Pre-existing on base (origin/feat/crafting-intepreters d4cfa3ac) — reproduced there directly.

Relevant to y9y4.9's typeck refactor: vtype_mentions_typeparam was widened (via ValueType.any) to detect T inside Union/Tuple/Newtype, which is correct, but the return-substitution/codegen path for those positions is separately incomplete — this ICE blocks an end-to-end test of that detection. Surfaced while preparing PR #10.

close_reason:
@expand(derive_walker) on core/ast.av's Pattern enum compiles, selfhosts, and consumer fixtures successfully call Pattern.children() on every variant shape (Wildcard/LitInt/Or/NestedVariant). Spec test walker_pattern_test.av passes via the standard test runner.

This is the FIRST real-AST-enum application of derive_walker — proves the macro infrastructure works through Module-wrapped cross-package imports. Required fixing 1pre (any_expand_annotation Module recursion) and the bind_params_ctx locals leak (6if1) along the way.

Path forward: ValueType (y9y4.6) is the next-smallest. Mostly direct/List-of-Self shapes. Stmt + Expr need wrapper-list shapes (y9y4.11) first.

description:
Generate `Type__map(self, f: (Type) -> Type) -> Type` — for each variant, recursively map f over child positions, rebuild the variant with transformed children.

## Sketch

```avra
// Generated by derive_walker:
fn Stmt__map(self: Stmt, f: (Stmt) -> Stmt) -> Stmt {
    match self {
        .Let(name, ty, init) -> .Let(name, ty, init)  // no Stmt children
        .If(c, t, e) -> .If(c, f(t), if e != null { f(e!) } else { null })
        // ...
    }
}
```

## Why

Many compiler passes are 'rewrite each Stmt by some local function and recurse' — map() captures that pattern exactly. desugar/mod.av and features/comptime/rewrite.av both spend most of their lines on this.

## Acceptance

- map() generated for each AST enum that gets @expand(derive_walker).
- Spec test: map(self, id) is identity. map(self, transform_one) replaces one node deterministically.

## Depends on

- y9y4.1 (Slice A) — shares the macro infrastructure, child-classification logic.

Reproducer:

fn make_counter() -> fn() -> int {
    mut count = 0
    () -> {
        count = count + 1
        count
    }
}

fn main() {
    let c = make_counter()
    eprintln(c())  // expected 1, got 105553132130753 (garbage)
}

Root cause: Capture record (features/closures/codegen.av:28) stores 'alloca: ptr' — a direct pointer to the enclosing scope's stack alloca. When the enclosing fn returns, the stack is gone. The closure still holds the pointer.

Fix: mutable captures need heap-promotion — allocate the binding on the heap when any closure captures it as mutable. Spec Axis 9 (memory model) calls for this. The current immutable-by-value capture path is fine, only mut needs promotion.

Repro:

    type T = { v: int }

    impl T {
        fn pair(self) -> (int, T) {
            (self.v, T { v: self.v + 1 })
        }
    }

    fn main() -> int {
        let t = T { v: 10 }
        let (n, t2) = t.pair()
        if t2.v == 11 { 0 } else { 1 }
    }

Crashes at codegen:

    codegen error: field access on non-struct value (ty=<unknown>, field=v, line=...)

The destructured `t2` has type Unknown even though `t.pair()` returns `(int, T)`. The same pattern works for tuple-returning FREE functions; it's specific to METHOD-call-result destructure.

## Where

`bind_destructure(tc, names, r.ty)` is called with `r.ty` from `check_expr(tc, init)`. For a `.Call` to an impl method, check_expr eventually routes through `check_method_call` which returns `r.ret_ty` (the FnTypeFound's ret_ty) untouched. If the method returns a tuple, that's preserved.

But annotate_sexpr's `.Call` fast-path returns `fn_type_get(tc.fns, name).ret_ty` — which for method calls uses the mangled name and may not be in the fns registry (impl methods are registered as `Type__method` not bare `method`). Returns Unknown. The let-destructure annotate path then re-runs check_expr but maybe with different state.

Likely fix: extend annotate_sexpr's `.Call(.FieldAccess(...), ...)` path to look up the mangled method name, or fall through to check_expr cleanly. Then destructure annotates with the right tuple_ty.

## Blocks

- nce6.3 (Result-returning reader: `let (v, r) = self.read_int()?` chained — even fragiler)
- @std/process `read_int_list` / `read_string_list` helpers (loop body destructures `r.read_int()` result and reuses `r` later)
- Likely a long tail of user code that does fluent method chains returning tuples

Red-team finding from vez6.8.3.

When a user writes:
  component Cmd implements Runnable {}

the parser threads 'Runnable' into ComponentDef.implements. Then the @expand macro (the macro author's @comptime fn) is responsible for generating one 'impl Runnable for Cmd_inst { ... }' block per declared trait. Today the typeck does NOT check that the macro actually did this — implements is a HINT, not a constraint.

Reproduction:
  @comptime fn expand_cmd(s: Stmt) -> Stmt { /* generates 'type Cmd_inst' but NO 'impl Runnable for Cmd_inst' */ }
  @expand(expand_cmd)
  component Cmd implements Runnable {}
  let c: dyn Runnable = Cmd_inst {}  // ← this fails with a method-not-found later

The user gets a downstream F1000 / F3000 instead of a focused 'F1xxx: macro for Cmd did not produce impl Runnable for any per-instance type'.

Fix shape:
- After expand_macros runs, walk every ComponentDef.implements and verify a post-expansion impl matching that trait exists for SOME per-instance type derived from this component. Today implements is a syntactic hint with no semantic enforcement.

Why not blocker: the symptom is a confusing error message, not a wrong-program-accepted bug. Filing as P2; ties into the broader 'trait conformance ergonomics' surface that ovec / twn0 covered for the post-expansion case.

Filed during vez6.8.3 cleanup C.

Splice syntax (`~name` and `~(expr)`) currently works in expression positions inside `quote { ... }` blocks but NOT in identifier positions like the name slot of `type X = { ... }` or fn declarations.

Repro:
```
@comptime
fn make_type(s: Stmt) -> Stmt {
    let type_name = "Command_build"
    return quote stmt {
        type ~type_name = { x: int }
    }
}
```

Parse fails at `type ~type_name`: 'expected type name'. The parser's `type` decl path expects a bare identifier; it doesn't recognise `~expr` as a deferred-evaluation form there.

This blocks vez6.8's per-instance type generation: the canonical macro pattern is `@expand(expand_command)` reading the instance name from the wrapped `ComponentBlock` and emitting `type Command_<instance> = ...`. Without identifier-position splice, every instance produces the same hardcoded type name (filed dkfa for the duplicate-type-decl issue this surfaces).

Workaround: emit the type via an Avra runtime helper that accepts the name as a string param, or generate the source as a string and re-parse. Both ugly.

Fix direction: extend the parser's identifier-position handling to accept `~ident` (splice-of-ident) and `~(expr)` (splice-of-expr returning string). The lower pass already has Splice handling for expressions; extend construct_type_decl etc. to use the splice expression directly as the name arg instead of `string_lit_expr(name)`.

Effort: half a day. Parser + lower changes are localised; tests for type-name splice + fn-name splice.

Currently `enum_value_to_expr` returns raw post-resolve AST nodes (Expr.Ident(name), Expr.Number(text), Expr.Binary(...)). The fold pass (run_comptime) splices the decoded Expr into surrounding code AFTER resolve_names already ran. So when a comptime fn like:

```avra
@comptime
fn ten_as_ident() -> Expr {
    return quote { foo }
}

fn main() {
    let e: Expr = ten_as_ident()  // fold target
}
```

folds, the call site becomes `let e: Expr = Expr.Ident("foo")` where Expr.Ident is the AST-level node — codegen reads it as an identifier reference and emits 'undefined variable foo'.

The fix: decoder should produce an Expr.EnumCtor AST node (the source-level construction form) so the surrounding code reads it as a literal-AST-construction expression. Each payload becomes an EnumCtor or primitive literal; lists wrap with sexpr_dummy.

For the existing tests (which inspect AST shape directly), match patterns need updating from `.Ident(name)` to `.EnumCtor("...Expr", "Ident", _)`.

Acceptance: end-to-end test where a comptime fn returns a quote-built Expr and the surrounding code uses the result at runtime (e.g. render_expr of the result prints the expected token shape).

Tracked the existing decoder rewrite under vez6.6 since it's part of the @expand foundation. After this fix, the second half of vez6.6.4 (auto-register vs span-wrapper passthrough) can be re-evaluated for whether the passthrough is sufficient or auto-register is still needed.

Pattern that produces malformed LLVM IR (dead code after a basic-block terminator):

  match cur {
      .End -> {}
      .Node(ss, next) -> {
          match ss.node {
              .Function(name, _, _, _, fn_body) -> if name == fn_name { return fn_body }
              //                                  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
              //                                  if-expr as match-arm value, with `return` inside
              _ -> {}
          }
      }
  }

The codegen emits something like:

  ife_then:
    ret ptr %fn_body          ; valid terminator
    store i64 0, ptr %ife_result  ; DEAD — block already returned
    br label %ife_end         ; second terminator — invalid IR

llc rejects it with "instruction expected to be numbered '%4' or greater" or, with different register state, "unknown field <X>" — register collision in the malformed block.

Workaround: wrap the match-arm body in {} block-stmt. This forces statement-flavor codegen which handles `return` correctly:

  .Function(name, _, _, _, fn_body) -> {
      if name == fn_name { return fn_body }
  }

Discovered: 2026-05-09 during vez6.2 cleanup pass. Spent ~30 minutes bisecting because the error messages don't point at the source location — they're LLC errors deep in the bootstrap pipeline.

Fix path:
1. The codegen for if-expr-as-match-arm-value should detect when the then-branch contains a `return` (or any other basic-block terminator) and not emit the post-branch store/br.
2. OR: lower if-expr-with-return uniformly through the same path as if-stmt — the stmt path already handles this correctly.

Tracking: this affected my refactor of features/eval/find_fn_body and ast_introspect's has_fn / find_fn_body; had to use the {} workaround.

Bootstrap parser rejects three closure-body forms that are valid Avra constructs in non-closure contexts.

**1. Typed-arrow closure with `||` in expression body**

  fn f(items: SymList) {
      sym_list_filter(items, (item: Sym) -> item.name == "x" || item.qualified == "y")
  }

Workaround: extract the body to a named helper fn that returns bool, then pass the helper as the closure.

**2. Typed-arrow closure with multi-statement body (let + if-else)**

  sym_list_filter(items, (item: Sym) -> {
      let q = item.qualified
      if q == "" { false } else { q.starts_with(prefix) }
  })

Workaround: extract to a hand-rolled while-true loop, OR push the multi-statement logic into a named fn.

**3. Bare `return X` inside braced single-line match arm in a HOF helper**

The pattern works at the top level of a fn body:

  match cur {
      .End -> {
          return acc
      }
      ...
  }

But inside a fn that's *called* by a HOF where the closure-arg parser has set certain expectations, the same form parse-fails downstream with cascading "expected expression" / "unexpected `}`" errors. The actual parser confusion is upstream of the reported line.

Reproducer: see the abandoned sym_list_find / sym_list_filter refactor in cleanup-round-2 (commit comment in 83a9b9e5).

Workaround: expand match arm bodies onto multiple lines so each statement is its own line, OR avoid HOF helpers when callers need the return early.

Impact: blocks the cleanest version of round-2's HOF refactor for SymList. Achievable wins for the simple-body case (closure body = single fn-call expression like `(item: Sym) -> item.exported`) work fine. Compound conditions and multi-line bodies fall over.

Likely cause: the closure-arg parser's expression terminator detection. When `||` appears, the parser may eagerly consume past the closing `)` of the enclosing call. Multi-statement bodies inside `{}` may interact with the same terminator confusion.

Fixing this unblocks the rest of cleanup-round-3 (forge-crafting-intepreters-supg).

When a package source file declares an `impl Trait for Type` whose methods return `Result<T, E>`, importing the package via `@std.<pkg>` produces a codegen failure: `undefined enum type `<test_module>::@std::<pkg>::Result``.

The resolver is qualifying the built-in `Result` enum as if it were a package-local declaration, presumably because the impl method signature is parsed inside the package's namespace and the resolver doesn't escape to the global `Result` declaration when the package gets wrapped by the test runner's Module.

Symptoms (reproduced while building std-lsp / ticket forge-crafting-intepreters-rnb9.1):
  packages/std-lsp/src/lsp.av:
    export trait Corpus {
        fn extract(self) -> Result<SymList, IoError>
    }
    export type EmptyCorpus = {}
    impl Corpus for EmptyCorpus {
        fn extract(self) -> Result<SymList, IoError> { Result.Ok(SymList.End) }
    }
  tests/foo_test.av:
    use @std.lsp.{...}
    spec "x" { then "x" { 1 == 1 } }   // doesn't even reference EmptyCorpus
  $ bs2 test
    → codegen error: undefined enum type `foo_test::@std::lsp::Result`

Note: the error fires even when the test does not reference the impl. The mere presence of the impl in the wrapped package is enough.

Workaround: don't define trait impls returning `Result<T,E>` inside @std packages. Provide pure-fn helpers instead, or define the impl in the consumer crate. std-lsp ticket .1 took the helper-fn route and filed this ticket instead.

Root cause likely in resolve/names.av — whatever path qualifies type names inside trait method signatures isn't checking the global scope before defaulting to the enclosing module path. Probably a fix in `resolve_type_name` that needs to special-case built-in generic types (`Result`, `List`) the same way it does primitive types (`int`, `string`).

When a package source file uses `///` doc comments and is imported via `@std.<pkg>`, the test runner's outer `Stmt.Module` wrapper plus the resolver produce a codegen failure: `unsupported statement type (tag=…)` for the doc-comment statement form.

Symptoms (reproduced while building std-lsp / ticket forge-crafting-intepreters-rnb9.1):
  packages/std-lsp/src/lsp.av:
    /// A 1-based file position.
    export type Pos = { line: int, col: int }
    export fn pos_new(line: int, col: int) -> Pos { Pos { line, col } }
  tests/foo_test.av:
    use @std.lsp.{pos_new}
    spec "x" { then "x" { let p = pos_new(1, 2); p.line == 1 } }
  $ bs2 test --filter foo
    → codegen error: unsupported statement type (tag=7571023594101803)

Replacing `///` with `//` makes the same package work. Direct `bs2 check` of the package alone passes — the failure only surfaces when the test runner wraps the package in its synthetic Module.

Workaround: avoid `///` inside @std packages until x35m.1 (annotation desugaring) lands, since that ticket reshapes how doc comments are represented anyway.

Root cause likely sits in the desugar/codegen path for whatever AST node `///` currently produces — it's stored on the surrounding stmt in a way that survives module wrapping but isn't pattern-matched in codegen's wrapped-stmt handler.

Closing this ticket should be subsumed by x35m.1 (which replaces `///` with `@doc` annotations) — leave open until x35m.1 lands and verify it fixes the reproducer.

Indexing a map with the [] operator crashes codegen with an ICE instead of either working or emitting a clean type error.

Repro (bare bs2 compile):
  fn main() {
      let m = {"a": 1}
      println(string(m["a"]))
  }
=> error[F9999]: internal compiler error during codegen: codegen error: indexing on non-string/list value (ty=Map<string, int>)

