A direct generic struct literal whose type param is inferable from a FIELD VALUE fails F1002 (cannot infer type parameter V in Box) when it appears inside a spec/then block (a module-wrapped test file), but the SAME literal infers fine at top level in a plain program.

Repro (FAILS) — a *_test.av containing:
  type Box<V> = { items: List<V> }
  fn use_box(b: Box<int>) -> int { b.items.length }
  spec "x" { then "t" { use_box(Box { items: [1,2,3] }) == 3 } }
=> F1002 at the Box { items: [1,2,3] } site, even though items: [1,2,3] is List<int> so V=int is determined by the field value.

Repro (WORKS) — the identical construction at top level: fn main() { let _ = use_box(Box { items: [1,2,3] }) } builds OK; and a return-annotated helper works in either context: fn box_of(xs: List<int>) -> Box<int> { Box { items: xs } } infers V=int from the return annotation.

So field-value-driven inference (infer_from_fields) is not running (or its result is dropped) for a struct literal in the module/then context, while the let/return-annotation expected-type path does run. NOT pf15 (phantom param appearing only in method sigs, via a struct-FIELD initializer) and NOT 53pl (self-construct). Surfaced writing ebp6/452k closure-receiver specs: those tests construct via a return-annotated box_of helper to dodge this (generic_nonlocal_receiver_test.av and generic_closure_param_receiver_test.av use box_of instead of a direct struct literal). Likely in typeck struct-literal inference under the deferred-init/module wrapping the test runner applies.

Two latent completeness gaps in the monomorphizer's expected-type threading, surfaced while red-teaming qcvb (PR #536). Neither is memory-unsafe (da7dee12's gap-fill + typeck check handle the safety axis); both are correctness/consistency gaps.

(1) CONST collect/rewrite asymmetry (features/generics/mono.av).
collect_inst_stmt line ~1157: '.Const(_, _, init) -> collect_inst_expr(init, ...)' discards the annotation (Unknown expected), while rewrite_stmt line ~2302 '.Const(n, vt, init) -> ...rewrite_expr_with_expected(init, vt, ...)' threads it. The two mono passes therefore disagree for a const generic-struct literal. Currently UNREACHABLE — typeck gates const to compile-time-constant expressions and rejects any struct literal ('const requires a compile-time constant expression'), so mono never sees a const struct lit. But the asymmetry is a latent footgun: if const restrictions ever loosen, collect would silently diverge from rewrite. Fix: make collect '.Const(_, vt, init) -> collect_inst_expr_with_expected(init, vt, ...)' to match .Let/.Mut and the rewrite side.

(2) Nested-struct-literal-in-field doesn't thread the field's declared type as expected.
Repro:
  type Arena<N, Id> = { nodes: List<N>, count: int }
  type Outer = { inner: Arena<int, int>, label: string }
  fn main() { let o: Outer = Outer { inner: Arena { nodes: [], count: 1 }, label: "x" } }
=> F1002 'cannot infer type parameter Id in Arena<...>'. The inner Arena construction's expected type should be the field's declared type (Arena<int,int>), but the .StructLit arm in collect_inst_expr_with_expected / rewrite_expr_with_expected recurses into field inits with Unknown. Loud + consistent (both passes agree → no silent miscompile), and out of qcvb's let/return scope, so not a regression. Fix: in the .StructLit expected arm, look up each field's declared type from the struct registry and thread it as the expected type for that field's initializer. Relates to epic so07 (typeck/mono foundations).

A then-description containing string interpolation (e.g. then "\"${v}\" interpolates" { ... }) is not supported. On a 2-then file it fails FAST with "error parsing <file>" (acceptable but the diagnostic does not say WHY or point at the ${...} in the description). On a larger file (8 thens, ~45 lines) the same construct made shard compile spin and get OOM-KILLED by the kernel (observed twice: "Killed" after 115s and 199s of a normally-sub-second compile) — error recovery appears to re-parse/accumulate unboundedly. Replacing the descriptions with plain text fixes it instantly (same body code).

FAST REPRO (parse error, no kill):
  fn show_i(v: int?) -> str { string(v) }
  spec "repro" {
      then "string(Some(5)) is \"5\"" { show_i(5) == "5" }
      then "\"${v}\" interpolates" {
          let v: int? = 7
          "v=${v}" == "v=7"
      }
  }
  → ./build/bs2 test <file> reports "shard compile failed: error parsing <file>".

KILL REPRO: the same construct inside a larger test file — see git history of features/null_safety/tests/optional_display_test.av (the pre-defang version in the optional-display fix commit) — ./build/bs2 test <file> is OOM-killed after ~2 min.

FIX SHAPE: (1) the spec/then description lexer should either support interpolation or reject it with a pointed F-code ("interpolation is not allowed in a spec/then description") at the ${ position; (2) whatever recovery loop re-scans after the malformed description must be bounded. TEST TO WRITE: a compile-error test via avra_shell_exec asserting the pointed F-code on the FAST repro, plus a timeout-guarded run asserting the large file errors in <5s rather than hanging.

